import * as THREE from 'three';
const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({
uniforms: {
uTime: { value: 0 },
uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(0x88ccff) },
},
vertexShader: /* glsl */ `
varying vec2 vUv;
void main() {
vUv = uv;
vec3 pos = position;
pos.y += sin(uv.x * 10.0 + 1.0) * 0.1; // wave
gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(pos, 1.0);
}
`,
fragmentShader: /* glsl */ `
uniform vec3 uColor;
uniform float uTime;
varying vec2 vUv;
void main() {
vec3 c = uColor + 0.2 * sin(uTime + vUv.x * 6.28);
gl_FragColor = vec4(c, 1.0);
}
`,
});
const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(4, 4, 64, 64), material);
scene.add(mesh);
// drive the uniform
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
material.uniforms.uTime.value = clock.getElapsedTime();
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
Key automatic globals ShaderMaterial injects for you:
projectionMatrix, modelViewMatrix, viewMatrix, normalMatrix — camera math.
position, normal, uv — attributes.
cameraPosition — uniform.
For RawShaderMaterial, none of these are injected; you declare every attribute + uniform manually. Useful for advanced custom pipelines; usually overkill.

GLSL ES 3.00 (WebGL2) syntax:
#version 300 es
precision highp float;
uniform float uTime;
uniform vec3 uColor;
uniform sampler2D uMap;
in vec3 position; // attribute
in vec2 uv;
out vec2 vUv; // varying
void main() {
vUv = uv;
gl_Position = vec4(position, 1.0);
}
Types:
Type · Notes
float, int, bool · scalars
vec2, vec3, vec4 · float vectors
mat3, mat4 · matrices
sampler2D, samplerCube · texture handles
Built-in math: sin, cos, mix(a, b, t), smoothstep(a, b, x), clamp(x, lo, hi), step(edge, x), length, normalize, dot, cross, texture(sampler, uv).
Coordinate spaces:
Space · What · Useful for
Local (model) · the geometry's own coords · wave deformations
World · after model matrix · global noise, world-space effects
View (camera) · after view matrix · fog, depth
Clip · after projection · the final gl_Position
NDC · clip ÷ w · screen-space effects

You usually don't want to rewrite PBR from scratch. To inject just one tweak into a MeshStandardMaterial:
const mat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xffffff });
mat.onBeforeCompile = (shader) => {
// add a uniform
shader.uniforms.uTime = { value: 0 };
// expose the uniform to the shader code
shader.vertexShader = `
uniform float uTime;
${shader.vertexShader}
`.replace(
'#include <begin_vertex>',
`
#include <begin_vertex>
transformed.y += sin(uv.x * 10.0 + uTime) * 0.1;
`
);
mat.userData.shader = shader; // keep a ref to update uniform later
};
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
if (mat.userData.shader) {
mat.userData.shader.uniforms.uTime.value = clock.getElapsedTime();
}
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
You inject GLSL snippets at named #include points in Three.js's shader source (begin_vertex, output_fragment, dithering_fragment, etc.). Read the relevant file in three/src/renderers/shaders/ShaderChunk/ to find the right hook.
This pattern keeps PBR lighting, shadows, env maps — all the heavy stuff — while you customise one part. Far simpler than rewriting a full standard material.

Three.js introduced TSL as stable in r184. You compose shaders in JavaScript using nodes that compile to GLSL (for WebGL2) or WGSL (for WebGPU) automatically.
Advantages:
One material source works on both backends.
Type-safe in TypeScript.
Composable — assign nodes to material properties; the renderer wires them up.
Easier to read than long GLSL strings.
import * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';
import { time, uv, vec3, mix, sin, positionLocal, mx_noise_float } from 'three/tsl';
const material = new THREE.MeshStandardNodeMaterial();
// wave the surface based on UV.x + time
material.positionNode = positionLocal.add(
vec3(0, sin(uv().x.mul(10).add(time)).mul(0.1), 0)
);
// procedural colour mixing
material.colorNode = mix(
vec3(0.1, 0.4, 1.0),
vec3(1.0, 0.6, 0.2),
uv().y
);
const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(4, 4, 64, 64), material);
scene.add(mesh);
time, uv(), positionLocal, cameraPosition etc. are built-in nodes. Mathematical operations (add, mul, sin, mix, smoothstep) chain naturally.

// in fragment shader
varying vec3 vNormal;
varying vec3 vViewPosition;
float fresnel = pow(1.0 - max(dot(normalize(vNormal), normalize(vViewPosition)), 0.0), 3.0);
vec3 rim = vec3(0.4, 0.6, 1.0) * fresnel;
gl_FragColor = vec4(baseColor + rim, 1.0);
TSL equivalent (sketch):
import { normalView, positionViewDirection, pow, max, dot, vec3 } from 'three/tsl';
const fresnel = pow(max(dot(normalView, positionViewDirection).oneMinus(), 0), 3);
material.emissiveNode = vec3(0.4, 0.6, 1.0).mul(fresnel);

A noise threshold; pixels below are discarded.
uniform float uDissolve;
uniform sampler2D uNoise;
varying vec2 vUv;
void main() {
float n = texture(uNoise, vUv * 4.0).r;
if (n < uDissolve) discard;
gl_FragColor = vec4(baseColor, 1.0);
}

uniform sampler2D uHeight;
uniform float uAmplitude;
void main() {
vec3 pos = position;
float h = texture(uHeight, uv).r;
pos.y += h * uAmplitude;
gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(pos, 1.0);
}

A second pass: render the model slightly inflated along normals as a back-faces-only black material → silhouette outline.
// vertex shader for the outline pass
void main() {
vec3 pos = position + normal * 0.02;
gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(pos, 1.0);
}
material.side = THREE.BackSide;
material.depthWrite = false;
material.color = new THREE.Color(0x000000);

WebGPU enables compute shaders — work that runs on the GPU outside the render pass. Use cases: particle simulation, GPU pathfinding, image processing, ML inference.
TSL exposes compute via computeNode:
import { Fn, instanceIndex, storage } from 'three/tsl';
const positionsBuffer = ...; // a storage buffer
const compute = Fn(() => {
const i = instanceIndex;
storage(positionsBuffer, 'vec3', i).x = sin(time.add(i)).mul(5);
})().compute(particleCount);
renderer.compute(compute); // run before render
This is advanced territory; the API is stabilising in 2026. For most projects, you won't need compute shaders for years.
